\details{
The model is a zero-mean, unit-variance Gaussian ARMA/SARMA for
the normal scores \code{qnorm(U)}, so only the ARMA coefficients are
estimated. Estimation is by BFGS on an unconstrained scale (hyperbolic
tangent of the partial autocorrelations), on which every parameter value
is stationary and invertible. If BFGS does not converge, Nelder-Mead is
tried from the BFGS value and the better result is kept.

\code{se = "hessian"} gives the inverse observed information, carried to the
coefficients by the delta method. It is quick but only approximate: for
pseudo-observations it ignores the variability of the ranks, and for PIT
data it ignores estimation of the margins. \code{se = "bootstrap"} is a
parametric bootstrap: \code{B} series are simulated from the fitted model
(exact stationary start, same missing-value pattern), ranked if
\code{pseudo = TRUE}, and refitted with the same starting rules as the
original fit (from \code{init} or \code{object} if given, otherwise from
Hannan-Rissanen values). The covariance is that of the replicates; a
replicate whose refit fails is recorded as \code{NA} and dropped.

For a \link[=dvinecop-class]{dvinecop} model the ARMA coefficients and the
auxiliary parameters (degrees of freedom of \code{t} copulas, \code{delta} of BB1
copulas) are estimated jointly, by BFGS on an unconstrained scale with a
Nelder-Mead fallback. Starting values come from \code{init}, otherwise from
\code{object} if its ARMA coefficients are specified, otherwise from a Gaussian
\link[=armacop-class]{armacop} fit to the data. The effective lag \code{K} is computed at the
starting values and held fixed during the optimisation; at the optimum it
is recomputed, and if the log-likelihood changes by 0.01 or more the model
is refitted with the larger \code{K} (at most three times; \code{K} never decreases
within a fit). The fitted object records the starting \code{K}, the number of
refits and the final log-likelihood change.

Free-mode models cannot be fitted. The data must be complete.
\code{se = "bootstrap"} is recommended: the Hessian standard errors are
unreliable where a rotated copula switches rotation (Kendall's tau near
zero), at the boundary of a BB1 copula's feasible region, and for
pseudo-observations.
